  m_Name: CrossFlowTurbineData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: CrossFlow Turbine
  engineDescription: A Cross Flow Turbine is a water turbine used to convert the
    kinetic and potential energy of flowing water into mechanical rotational energy,
    with the water passing through the runner in a direction transverse to its rotational
    axis. Its main components include the nozzle, guide mechanism, cylindrical runner,
    curved blades, shaft, casing, and bearings. During operation, water from the
    penstock enters the turbine through a nozzle, which converts pressure energy
    into a high-velocity water jet. The jet strikes the curved blades of the rotating
    runner and transfers energy to the runner. The water then passes through the
    runner a second time, transferring additional energy before leaving the turbine.
    Cross-flow turbines are widely used in small and micro-hydroelectric power plants,
    particularly where water flow conditions vary significantly. From an engineering
    perspective, understanding hydraulic efficiency, nozzle design, blade geometry,
    water velocity, runner speed, head and flow rate, cavitation, mechanical losses,
    and maintenance is essential for achieving efficient and reliable power generation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
